WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.412
There are four basic relational terms that are useful to translate into everyday language.

00:00:05.412 --> 00:00:10.212
A relation is essentially a table about one subject or event type.

00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:13.247
A tuple is one row in that table.

00:00:13.247 --> 00:00:16.892
An attribute is a column that describes each row.

00:00:16.892 --> 00:00:21.312
A domain describes the allowed type and rules for a value.

00:00:21.312 --> 00:00:25.042
For example, the patient table is the relation.

00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:27.052
One patient is a tuple.

00:00:27.052 --> 00:00:29.172
Birth date is an attribute.

00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:33.627
The allowed type and constraints on birth date are part of its domain.

00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:36.282
One important word is atomic.

00:00:36.282 --> 00:00:38.917
Atomic does not mean physically small.

00:00:38.917 --> 00:00:42.322
It means the database treats the field as one value.

00:00:42.322 --> 00:00:48.942
If a column contains a comma-separated list of service codes and each service needs to be searched, joined,

00:00:48.942 --> 00:00:54.832
or validated separately, that list is not a good atomic design for the relational model.

00:00:54.832 --> 00:01:00.672
Before drawing tables, write one sentence that defines what one row represents.

00:01:00.672 --> 00:01:04.197
That grain statement prevents many later problems.

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:10.817
If one row represents a patient, repeated encounters do not belong as extra columns in the same row;

00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:13.896
they are a different entity with a one-to-many relationship.
